# Matter and the Atom: An Introduction to Chemistry

> Chemistry is the study of matter — what it's made of, how it's
> structured, and how it changes. Everything starts with the atom.

## 1. Matter and Its States

[definition]
**Matter** is anything that has mass and takes up space. It exists in
three everyday states — **solid** (fixed shape, particles vibrate in
place), **liquid** (fixed volume, flowing particles), and **gas** (no
fixed shape or volume, fast free particles) — plus plasma at high
temperatures.
[/definition]

Changes of state are physical, not chemical:

| Change | Direction | Term |
| --- | --- | --- |
| solid → liquid | melting | heat in |
| liquid → gas | evaporation / boiling | heat in |
| gas → liquid | condensation | heat out |
| liquid → solid | freezing | heat out |

## 2. Inside the Atom

| Particle | Charge | Mass (relative) | Location |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Proton | +1 | 1 | nucleus |
| Neutron | 0 | 1 | nucleus |
| Electron | −1 | ~1/1836 | orbiting shells |

- **Atomic number** = number of protons. It defines the element.
- **Mass number** = protons + neutrons.
- **Isotopes** are atoms of one element differing in neutron count
  (carbon-12 vs carbon-14).

## Example

A neutral sodium atom has atomic number 11 → 11 protons, so also 11
electrons. Its most common isotope has mass number 23:

@math label="eq:sodium-neutrons"
23 - 11 = 12 \text{ neutrons}
@/math

## 3. Elements, Compounds, Mixtures

@diagram tree id="matter-classification" layout="tree" aria="Classification of matter"
  Matter -> Element
  Matter -> Compound
  Matter -> Mixture
  Mixture -> "Homogeneous (solutions)"
  Mixture -> "Heterogeneous"
@/diagram

- **Element:** one type of atom only (oxygen, gold).
- **Compound:** two+ elements chemically bonded in fixed ratios (H₂O,
  CO₂). Properties differ utterly from the elements inside — soft,
  reactive sodium + poisonous green chlorine = table salt.
- **Mixture:** substances combined but *not* bonded; separable by
  physical means (filtering, distilling).

[important title="Chemical vs physical change"]
Tearing paper, dissolving sugar, melting ice — no new substances form:
*physical*. Burning paper creates ash, CO₂, water — new substances:
*chemical*. The test is always "did the identity change?"
[/important]

## 4. Why Atoms Bond

Atoms bond to reach stable outer electron shells:

- **Ionic bonds** — electrons transferred (metal + non-metal, e.g. NaCl)
- **Covalent bonds** — electrons shared (non-metals, e.g. H₂O)
- **Metallic bonds** — electron "sea" around positive ions (conductivity!)

## Key Takeaways

- States of matter differ by particle arrangement and energy.
- Protons define the element; isotopes vary only in neutrons.
- Compounds are chemically bonded in fixed ratios; mixtures aren't.
